{"number": 19, "text": "                    play,     grow,       splash\n                    Consonant             clusters         at   the     beginning            of    words\n\n                 1  Combinations   of consonant sounds  (consonant  clusters) can be difficult to pronounce for some\n                    learners. English words can start with a vowel, or one, two or three consonant sounds.\n                    Compare:\n\n                           am\n                                        ram\n                                                      cram\n                                                                      scram\n\n                    Here are the possible two-consonant  clusters at the start of English words:\n\n                                  jith\n                            play    x     class | black} x     glass   x      x     fly     x      x      x      x\n                     +/r/ | pray | trip | crime|brown|  drop | grow |  x      x     fry     x  | three           x\n                     +/w/ |   x  |                x             x      x      x      x      x      x      x      x\n                     +/j/ | pure | tube | queue|beauty| due     x    |music} news | few |  view    x      x    huge\n\n                    In addition, the following two-consonant  clusters are possible with /s/:\n                                                                  slow\n                                                    sphere         MV           swim\n                                                          /f/               /w/\n\n\n                                               snow   /n/                       /p/  spot\n\n\n                                                   smile                       star\n                                                                   /k/\n                                                                  sky\n\n                    Here are the possible three-consonant clusters at the start of English words:\n\n                                                  /st/          /sk/\n                                   splash         x             x\n                     +/r/          spray          straw         scream\n                     +/w/          x              x             squeak\n                     +/j/          x              stew          skewer\n\n                    Note: Some consonant  clusters marked x in A and B are used in a few uncommon\n                    words, for example. schwa (the name of the sound /a/) and people's names.\n\n                    In order to be understood clearly you should —\n                    e avoid changing  a consonant in a cluster to a different consonant.\n                      For example:  saying ‘present’ for ‘pleasant’ or saying ‘queue’ for ‘crew’\n                    e avoid leaving out one of the consonant  sounds.\n                      For example:  saying ‘poblem’ for ‘problem’ or saying ‘foo’ for ‘few’\n                    e avoid adding  an extra vowel between  consonants.\n                      For example:  saying ‘tewin’ for ‘twin’ or saying ‘faree’ for ‘free’\n                    e avoid adding  an extra vowel at the beginning of the word.\n                      For example:  saying ‘estop’ for ‘stop’ or saying ‘escream’ for ‘scream’\n\n                    You  can find more practice of consonant clusters at the beginning of words in Section E2.\n\n                       English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3438, "mean_conf": 87.93, "word_count": 337, "words": [{"text": "play,", "x0": 381, "y0": 135, "x1": 540, "y1": 217, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "grow,", "x0": 575, "y0": 153, "x1": 767, "y1": 218, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "splash", "x0": 803, "y0": 134, "x1": 1026, "y1": 217, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Consonant", "x0": 379, "y0": 228, "x1": 771, "y1": 292, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "clusters", "x0": 804, "y0": 229, "